<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03Q0">Definition <span data-tag="03Q0">4.9.1.6</span>.</a> Let $(S, \leq )$ be a linearly ordered set. We say that $(S, \leq )$ is <em>well-ordered</em> if it is well-founded when regarded as a partially ordered set: that is, if every nonempty subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ contains a smallest element. In this case, we will refer to the relation $\leq $ as a <em>well-ordering</em> of the set $S$. </p>
